  "hero.kicker": "Physics · Electromagnetism & Waves · An Essay You Can Play With",
  "hero.title": "What Travels Through Empty Space",
  "hero.sub": "With neither air nor water nor a single copper wire,<br>how do phone signals and sunlight cross hundreds of millions of kilometres to reach you?",
  "hero.lead": "Sound requires air to vibrate; ocean waves require water to churn. Yet between the Sun and Earth lies 150 million kilometres of vacuum; between Voyager 1 and ground stations lie more than 25 billion kilometres of interstellar space. There is almost no matter there, yet signals and light travel across at 299,792,458 metres per second, heating solar panels and streaming data into your phone. This essay answers just one question: <strong>when travelling through empty space, what is actually propagating forward on its own?</strong> Below are ten interactive models built on real physics equations or numerical solvers, walking step-by-step from the very first field line to 2026 frontiers in communications and lithography.",
  "hero.note": "Written by an AI, this page follows a quantitative physics progression from early-college to advanced levels while explaining the intuition behind each term in plain language. All physical constants follow 2019 SI / CODATA 2022; empirical measurements and frontier records cite their cutoff dates (November 2026) and sources; each chapter explicitly notes what its model leaves out.",

  /* ── 01 ── */
  "ch1.title": "Fields are real states of space, and news has a speed limit",
  "ch1.law": "Charges and currents do not pull on each other instantly across empty space; they first reshape the electric and magnetic fields around them. The news of that reshaping spreads outward at the finite speed c, flinging kinks into the distance.",
  "ch1.p1": "If you shake a magnet across the room or nudge a charged pith ball, common intuition suggests that a distant charge feels it instantly. As we saw in <a href=\"../electric-fields/#step0\">Electric Fields · Drawing an Invisible Force</a> and <a href=\"../magnetic-force/#step0\">Magnetic Force · The Force Is Always Sideways to the Velocity</a>, every point in space can be assigned a vector telling you exactly how much force a test charge placed there would experience.",
  "ch1.p2": "When you kick a charge so it accelerates to a velocity v = βc within a brief interval τ, something astonishing happens: space outside radius r &gt; ct does not yet know the charge has moved, so its field lines still point to the old position; space inside radius r &lt; c(t−τ) has already caught up to the new position. Trapped between them is a spherical shell of thickness cτ. To avoid snapping, every radial field line must make a transverse kink inside this shell—and that is <strong>the embryo of radiation</strong>.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Experiment 01 · A Kicked Charge: Field-Line Kinks and the 1/r Transverse Field",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag the final velocity β = v/c and acceleration time τ, or switch between \"Causal delay c\" and hypothetical \"Instantaneous propagation\", and click \"Kick again\" to watch the kink shell propagate outward.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Diagram of radial field lines forming transverse kinks after a charge accelerates, propagating outward at the speed of light",
  "ch1.leg.lines": "Electric field lines",
  "ch1.leg.shell": "Wavefront r = ct",
  "ch1.leg.eperp": "Transverse kink component E⊥",
