  "ch1.cv.instant": "Instantaneous: no kink, no wave",
  "ch1.say.causal": "At θ = 90° and r = {r} cτ₀, the transverse component E⊥ is <em>{ratio} times</em> the radial field Eᵣ: while the radial electrostatic field decays steeply as 1/r², the kink stretches with radius so that E⊥ falls only as 1/r.",
  "ch1.say.instant": "If information could travel across the universe instantaneously without delay, all field lines would simply translate together, with zero kink thickness—<em>producing no transverse electromagnetic wave at all</em>, and energy flux would vanish into 1/r⁴ nearby.",
  "ch1.p3": "This geometric argument, introduced into classic textbooks by J. J. Thomson and E. M. Purcell, explains it all: the static radial field Eᵣ ∝ 1/r² ties the field to the charge, while the transverse shear component of the kink obeys <strong>E⊥ = (q a sinθ) / (4πε₀ c² r)</strong>. When β = 0.20 and τ = 1.0, by the time the wavefront reaches r = 10 cτ₀, the transverse field in the perpendicular direction (θ = 90°) has already grown to <strong>2.0 times</strong> the radial static field.",
  "ch1.p4": "Why is this difference between 1/r² and 1/r a matter of life and death? Because energy density is proportional to the square of field strength. The radial field's energy flux decays as 1/r⁴, vanishing rapidly and never reaching interstellar space; but because E⊥ ∝ 1/r, its square decays as 1/r², exactly cancelling the expansion of the spherical area 4πr²—so the energy carried by that kink never fades out along the way.",
  "ch1.limit": "Model boundaries: This model uses the pulse-kink geometry under the non-relativistic low-speed approximation (β ≪ 1) to illustrate how 1/r² separates from 1/r; ultra-relativistic charges moving near the speed of light exhibit forward beaming (synchrotron radiation).",

  /* ── 02 ── */
  "ch2.title": "Changing magnetic fields create electric fields, and changing electric fields create magnetic fields",
  "ch2.law": "Faraday discovered that changing magnetic fields induce electric fields, and Maxwell completed the circle with displacement current: changing electric fields induce magnetic fields. Once linked head-to-tail, empty space itself becomes a self-sustaining loop.",
  "ch2.p1": "As you learned in <a href=\"../electromagnetic-induction/#step0\">Electromagnetic Induction · Change the Flux, Get a Voltage</a>, plunging a magnet into a coil produces an electromotive force whenever magnetic flux changes—even without a wire, a swirling vortex electric field E appears in space. But consider the reverse: cut a wire in half, solder on a pair of parallel metal plates to make a capacitor with pure vacuum in between. Why can an alternating current still cross it?",
  "ch2.p2": "In 1861, Maxwell realised: no electrons cross the gap between the plates, but the electric field E is changing back and forth. If <strong>ε₀ ∂E/∂t</strong> in empty space can encircle itself with a magnetic field B just like a real conduction current I_c, then an Ampèrian loop catches conduction current around the wire and an identical \"displacement current\" across the vacuum gap, seamlessly joining the magnetic field at both locations. Writing all experimental facts of electricity and magnetism into four integral equations, the physical meaning of each is just one sentence:",
  "ch2.eq1": "<strong>Gauss's Law for Electricity:</strong> The total electric flux through any closed surface equals the enclosed charge divided by ε₀—charges are the sources of electric fields.",
  "ch2.eq2": "<strong>Gauss's Law for Magnetism:</strong> The total magnetic flux through any closed surface is always 0—magnetic field lines always close on themselves in loops, with no isolated magnetic monopoles.",
  "ch2.eq3": "<strong>Faraday's Law of Induction:</strong> The circulation of the electric field around any closed loop equals the negative rate of change of magnetic flux enclosed by that loop.",
  "ch2.eq4": "<strong>Ampère-Maxwell Law:</strong> The circulation of the magnetic field around any closed loop equals the conduction current passing through it plus the vacuum displacement current ε₀ dΦ_E/dt.",
  "ch2.model.title": "Experiment 02 · Displacement Current in a Vacuum Gap and High-Frequency Induction",
  "ch2.model.how": "Drag the AC driving frequency f, plate radius R, and relative permittivity εᵣ to observe how conduction current I_c and vacuum displacement current I_d match perfectly, and how Faraday induction rears up at high frequencies.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Cross section of parallel plate capacitor with displacement current in gap inducing azimuthal magnetic field and waveform plot",
