  "ch2.say.quasi": "Capacitance C = {c} pF: No matter how you adjust it, I_d always precisely matches I_c (peak <em>{ic} A</em>), inducing a <em>{b} μT</em> azimuthal magnetic field at the rim; here kR = {kr}, and the Faraday feedback correction is only {far}%, behaving as an ideal lumped capacitor.",
  "ch2.say.wave": "Notice when frequency is pushed to <em>{f} MHz</em>, kR = {kr}: the gap's azimuthal magnetic field changes so fast that it triggers Faraday's law in reverse, inducing a <em>{far}%</em> extra radial electric field correction—the capacitor is no longer quiet, but radiating electromagnetic waves into space!",
  "ch2.say.nonpert": "Push frequency to <em>{f} MHz</em> where kR = {kr} (&gt; 2), and first-order (kR)²/4 = {far}% exceeds 100%—the lumped perturbative approximation breaks down as full Bessel/cavity waves take over and radiate outward!",
  "ch2.nonpert": "non-pert.",
  "ch2.p3": "Under default parameters (R = 5 cm, vacuum gap d = 5 mm, peak voltage 100 V), the capacitance is only <strong>13.9 pF</strong>. At 10 MHz, peak displacement current and conduction current are both <strong>0.087 A</strong>, and the Faraday feedback correction is a mere <strong>0.003%</strong>; but push the frequency to 1000 MHz (1 GHz, where wavelength shrinks to 0.30 m), and both peak currents leap to <strong>8.74 A</strong> while Faraday feedback surges to <strong>27.5%</strong>—quasistatic circuit theory breaks down completely, leaving full Maxwellian waves.",
  "ch2.limit": "Model boundaries: The Faraday feedback ratio is estimated using the first-order Bessel correction (kR)²/4 for circular parallel plates; true high-frequency radiation also requires calculating open-boundary radiation resistance (Chapter 4).",

  /* ── 03 ── */
  "ch3.title": "The wave is not put in by hand; it falls straight out of the equations",
  "ch3.law": "In a vacuum free of charge and conduction current, Faraday's law and displacement current take mutual derivatives to yield the wave equation directly—locking the wave speed firmly to two electromagnetic constants at c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀).",
  "ch3.p1": "Imagine a plane wave travelling along the x-direction, with its electric field pointing along the y-direction E_y(x,t) and magnetic field pointing along the z-direction B_z(x,t). In vacuum where there is no charge density ρ and no conduction current I_c, write Faraday's law and the Ampère-Maxwell law in one-dimensional differential form, then eliminate one field between x and t:",
  "ch3.eq.lbl": "Eliminating B_z from the first-order coupled equations yields the second-order wave equation directly:",
  "ch3.p2": "Three unshakeable rules emerge at once: first, E_y, B_z, and the propagation direction x are mutually perpendicular, making electromagnetic waves purely <strong>transverse waves</strong>; second, in any vacuum plane wave, E_y / B_z = c; third, the wave speed needs no elastic modulus or density of an \"aether\"—it is simply the reciprocal square root of the product of the vacuum permittivity ε₀ and permeability μ₀, which CODATA 2022 values evaluate to exactly <strong>299,792,458 m/s</strong>. What you see below is not an animated graphic, but a <strong>1-D Yee leapfrog Finite-Difference Time-Domain (FDTD) solver</strong> used in real electromagnetic engineering for antennas and chips: magnetic fields on half-integer grids update from spatial differences of earlier electric fields, and electric fields on integer grids update from spatial differences of the newly updated magnetic fields.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Experiment 03 · 1-D Yee FDTD Leapfrog Wavepacket and a Glass Interface",
  "ch3.model.how": "Adjust the refractive index n of the right-hand medium (e.g. 1.5 for glass) and the Courant number S = cΔt/Δx, or toggle between pulse and continuous wave. Watch how hitting the interface splits the pulse into reflected and transmitted waves, and see numerical instability explode when S > 1.",
  "ch3.model.aria": "1-D Yee FDTD simulation of electric and magnetic fields in leapfrog propagation and Fresnel reflection and transmission at dielectric interface",
