  "ch3.cv.x": "Spatial grid x",
  "ch3.cv.y": "Normalized field",
  "ch3.say.unstable": "Warning: Courant number S = {s} &gt; 1! The numerical update step travels faster than the physical speed of light, failing to enclose the true causal cone; electric field amplitude is exploding exponentially (peak reached <em>{pk}</em>).",
  "ch3.say.cw": "After entering the n = {n} medium, the continuous wave slows down to <em>{v} c</em> and its wavelength compresses proportionally to 1/{n}; on the left, the incident wave superimposes with the <em>{rth}%</em> reflected wave to form a partial standing wave.",
  "ch3.say.pulse": "At the n = {n} interface, normal-incidence Fresnel theory predicts <em>{rth}%</em> reflection and {tth}% transmission; the FDTD leapfrog pulse actually splits into <em>{rm}%</em> reflection and {tm}% transmission, while wavelength and speed inside the medium both shrink to <em>{v} c</em>, preserving total energy at a ratio of {u}.",
  "ch3.p3": "Setting S to exactly 1.00 advances the pulse in vacuum by precisely 1 cell per step with zero numerical dispersion; crossing the n = 1.5 glass interface, theoretical normal-incidence Fresnel reflectivity is R = ((n−1)/(n+1))² = <strong>4.0%</strong> and transmittance is T = 4n/(n+1)² = <strong>96.0%</strong>, with pulse speed dropping to c/n = <strong>0.667 c</strong>. In unit tests, this 240-cell FDTD agrees with analytical values within half a percentage point, conserving total energy to better than 99.99% in lossless media. For an intuitive ray-tracing comparison of refraction and interfaces, see <a href=\"../../optics/reflection-refraction/#step0\">Reflection & Refraction · How Light Bends</a>.",
  "ch3.p4": "Why does light slow down in glass? Photons are not bouncing zig-zag off tiny glass spheres; rather, the wave's electric field drives bound electrons in the glass into oscillation, and those electrons radiate secondary waves at the same frequency that interfere back into the beam, retarding the net phase velocity to v = c/n (the exact same driven-oscillator dispersion found in <a href=\"../../optics/why-the-sky-is-blue/#ch2\">Why Is the Sky Blue?</a>).",
  "ch3.limit": "Model boundaries: This solver assumes a dispersionless, non-absorbing 1-D linear non-magnetic medium (μᵣ = 1) with first-order Mur absorbing boundary conditions (ABC); once the pulses leave the computational domain boundaries, the left/right energy ratio no longer represents interface partition.",

  /* ── 04 ── */
  "ch4.title": "Only accelerating charges fling waves into space",
  "ch4.law": "A stationary charge has a field but no wave; a uniformly moving charge carries its field along with it. Only acceleration a flings the 1/r transverse kink from Chapter 1 out to infinity, with radiated power proportional to a².",
  "ch4.p1": "Recall the kink formula from Chapter 1: E⊥ = (q a sinθ)/(4πε₀ c² r). Multiplying energy density by the speed of light and integrating over the entire spherical surface yields the Larmor formula: <strong>P = q² a² / (6π ε₀ c³)</strong>. Notice the <strong>sin²θ</strong> term: looking directly down the axis along which the electron oscillates (θ = 0° and 180°), you see no transverse motion at all, so the kink is zero and radiation is zero; all radiated power concentrates toward the equatorial plane perpendicular to the acceleration axis (θ = 90°).",
  "ch4.p2": "When a charge oscillates harmonically like a spring at frequency f, its acceleration is proportional to f², meaning total radiated power is proportional to <strong>f⁴</strong> (or <strong>1/λ⁴</strong>)—the very core of Rayleigh scattering in <a href=\"../../optics/why-the-sky-is-blue/#ch2\">Why Is the Sky Blue?</a>: higher-frequency blue light shakes electrons in air molecules far more vigorously, re-radiating vastly more power than red light. If you slice a piece of metal wire down the middle and feed both ends with alternating current to make a <strong>dipole antenna</strong>, the oscillating currents along the wire interfere and superimpose, shaping the radiation pattern and efficiency:",
  "ch4.model.title": "Experiment 04 · Dipole Radiation Pattern, Directivity, and Half-Wave Resonance",
  "ch4.model.how": "Drag the ratio of antenna length to wavelength L/λ (or click presets) and the observation angle θ to see how pattern lobes, directivity D, and radiation resistance R_rad evolve from a short dipole to a multi-lobe antenna.",
