  "ch4.say.main": "At L = {l}λ, radiation resistance referenced to current antinode is <em>{r} Ω</em>, maximum directivity is <em>{d} ({dbi} dBi)</em>, and half-power beamwidth is {bw}°. At observation angle θ = {ang}°, power is <em>{rel}%</em> of peak; {note}",
  "ch4.note.short": "radiation resistance for an electrically short dipole drops below 1 Ω, almost entirely lost to ohmic wire heating.",
  "ch4.note.half": "the feed point of a half-wave dipole sits right at a current antinode; its 73.1 Ω is an almost natural match to common 75 Ω coaxial cable, making it radio's classic reference antenna.",
  "ch4.note.full": "extending length to a full wavelength narrows the beam further (48°), but the centre feed coincides with a current node, causing input impedance to skyrocket.",
  "ch4.note.multi": "beyond 1.25λ the main lobe splits into multiple sidelobes, throwing most energy obliquely rather than into the equator.",
  "ch4.p3": "This is why phone antennas cannot be made arbitrarily short, and why base stations and radio whips gravitate toward λ/2 or λ/4 (using a metallic ground plane to mirror a full half-wave): a short 0.1λ dipole has an antinode radiation resistance of only <strong>0.19 Ω</strong> (and only ~1.97 Ω at the triangular-current feed terminal), whereas a <strong>0.5λ half-wave dipole</strong> lifts radiation resistance cleanly to <strong>73.1 Ω</strong>, with directivity <strong>1.64 (2.15 dBi)</strong> and half-power beamwidth <strong>78.5°</strong>, launching energy cleanly out of the metal and into vacuum.",
  "ch4.limit": "Model boundaries: Radiation patterns and radiation resistance assume sinusoidal current distributions on thin, center-fed straight wires, omitting ohmic wire loss, feedline reflections, and coupling to nearby ground or human bodies.",

  /* ── 05 ── */
  "ch5.title": "Waves carry more than just shapes: energy, momentum, and polarization",
  "ch5.law": "Electromagnetic waves carry energy (Poynting vector S = E×B/μ₀) and momentum (radiation pressure I/c) across vacuum; meanwhile, their transverse orientation of vibration—polarization—decides whether they pass through a fence.",
  "ch5.p1": "Chapter 4 showed that accelerating charges throw off power P. As this power spreads across a sphere of radius r, the energy flux density per unit area is the <strong>Poynting vector</strong> S = (E×B)/μ₀, with magnitude equal to intensity I(r) = P/(4πr²). At Earth's orbit (1 AU), sunlight delivers a steady flux of about <strong>1361 W/m²</strong> (corresponding to an RMS electric field of about 716 V/m). Even more wondrous: waves carry not only energy, but also momentum p = U/c.",
  "ch5.p2": "Momentum hitting an object produces radiation pressure: a perfectly absorbing black surface feels a force of <strong>I/c</strong>, while a perfectly reflecting mirror bounces light back along its path, doubling the momentum transfer and doubling radiation pressure to <strong>2I/c</strong>. Though faint, in the drag-free void of space this force relentlessly accelerates solar sails day and night. Meanwhile, because E is transverse to the direction of propagation, it gains another degree of freedom: <strong>polarization</strong>. Place two polarizers at 90° to each other and light is blocked completely; yet insert a third polarizer at 45° between them, and the darkened region lights up again!",
  "ch5.model.title": "Experiment 05 · Solar Sail Radiation Pressure (Top) and the Three-Polarizer Paradox (Bottom)",
  "ch5.model.how": "In the top panel, drag solar distance r and sail reflectivity R; in the bottom panel, toggle \"Insert middle polarizer P₂\" and drag angle θ₂ to watch transmitted intensity decompose and rebuild according to Malus's law cos²θ.",
