  "ch5.say.all": "At {au} AU with reflectivity R = {r}, flux S = {s} W/m² and radiation pressure is <em>{p} μPa</em>, accelerating a 100 m, 50 kg light sail by <em>{dv} m/s</em> per day. Polarization path below: {polmsg}",
  "ch5.pol.off": "Without the middle filter, vertical polarizer P₁ (0°) and horizontal polarizer P₃ (90°) are strictly orthogonal, yielding <em>0%</em> transmission.",
  "ch5.pol.zero": "With the {ang}° polarizer aligned parallel to P₁ or P₃, the final projection sin²θ₂ vanishes, keeping transmission at <em>0.0%</em>.",
  "ch5.pol.on": "With the {ang}° polarizer inserted, the 50% passed by P₁ projects onto {ang}° (leaving {i2}% of initial unpolarized intensity), then onto 90° (× sin²θ₂), allowing <em>{i3}%</em> through the crossed pair!",
  "ch5.p3": "At 1 AU, sunlight radiation pressure on a perfect blackbody is only <strong>4.54 μPa</strong>, and <strong>9.08 μPa</strong> on a perfect mirror—roughly equivalent to the weight of a sesame seed resting on a two-metre square sheet of paper. Yet for an ultra-thin 100 m × 100 m polyimide reflective sail with areal density 5 g/m² (total mass 50 kg), this steady 0.091 N thrust consumes not a single drop of fuel, accumulating <strong>156.9 m/s</strong> of velocity change every single day (1.57 km/s every ten days).",
  "ch5.p4": "Three polarizers prove even more directly that electromagnetic waves are transverse vectors: light is not filtered like black-or-white pellets; rather, the electric field vector E undergoes a <strong>geometric projection</strong> along the transmission axis (intensity ∝ E² ∝ cos²θ). Inserting a 45° P₂ in the middle first projects 50% of vertical polarized light into a 45° state at 1/√2 amplitude (leaving 25% intensity), and that diagonal vector then projects onto 90°, retaining half of its power—yielding a total transmittance of <strong>12.5%</strong> (or 1/8 of the original unpolarized light).",
  "ch5.limit": "Model boundaries: Solar sail calculations assume normal incidence to the Sun and neglect solar-wind particle pressure (roughly 1,000 times smaller than photon pressure); polarizers are treated as ideal lossless linear polarizers.",

  /* ── 06 ── */
  "ch6.title": "From radio to gamma rays is one single spectrum, but classical waves hit a wall at high frequencies",
  "ch6.law": "Whether the wavelength is thirty kilometres or smaller than an atomic nucleus, in vacuum they are all the exact same electromagnetic wave obeying c = λf; what truly reshapes reality is that single-photon energy E = hf crosses thermal noise and chemical bond thresholds at high frequencies.",
  "ch6.p1": "Sweeping frequency f from 10 kHz all the way to 10²⁰ Hz, not a single Maxwell equation changes in vacuum—what changes is wavelength λ = c/f and its comparison against the various yardsticks of the material world: antenna dimensions, rotational drag of water molecules, vibrational levels of gas molecules, all the way to molecular ionization thresholds.",
  "ch6.p2": "Crucially, classical Maxwellian theory allows wave amplitude to be scaled arbitrarily small and energy divided continuously; yet the real world hits a quantum wall at high frequencies—<strong>E = hf</strong>. When each energy packet hf is far smaller than room-temperature thermal agitation k_B T (about <strong>0.0259 eV</strong> at 300 K, corresponding to a crossover frequency of <strong>6.25 THz</strong>), a 1 W/m² beam packs trillions of in-phase photons, behaving as a smooth classical field; once past terahertz into infrared, visible, and X-rays, there is not even a single photon per λ²·(1/f) phase-space cell, forcing detectors and chemical bonds to absorb energy packet by discrete packet (see <a href=\"../../modern/photoelectric-effect/#step0\">The Photoelectric Effect · Light Comes in Packets</a>, <a href=\"../../modern/blackbody-radiation/#step0\">Blackbody Radiation · Why Hot Things Glow</a>, and <a href=\"../../modern/double-slit/#step0\">The Double-Slit Experiment · Single Particles Still Build Fringes</a>).",
  "ch6.model.title": "Experiment 06 · The Full Electromagnetic Spectrum, Classical-Quantum Boundary, and Dielectric Loss of Water",
  "ch6.model.how": "Drag the frequency slider or click scenario presets below to view wavelength, photon energy, comparison against room-temperature thermal energy k_B T (300 K) and the 10 eV ionization threshold, along with liquid water's Debye loss curve across microwave and millimeter-wave bands.",
  "ch6.model.aria1": "Logarithmic full electromagnetic spectrum from 10^4 to 10^20 Hz with photon energy comparison",
  "ch6.model.aria2": "Debye dielectric loss and penetration depth of liquid water from 0.5 to 100 GHz",
