  "ch6.band.xray": "X-ray band",
  "ch6.band.gamma": "gamma-ray band",
  "ch6.reg.class": "in the non-ionizing classical regime: individual photons cannot break chemical bonds; energy accumulates only through macroscopic Joule or dielectric heating",
  "ch6.reg.cross": "near the 6.25 THz thermal boundary where classical fields transition into granular photon behavior",
  "ch6.reg.quant": "in the quantum photon regime below the ~10 eV ionization threshold (visible/near-IR can excite electronic transitions or the photoelectric effect)",
  "ch6.reg.ion": "above the ~10 eV ionization threshold (EUV / X-ray / gamma): a single photon packs enough punch to knock electrons out of molecules and sever DNA bonds",
  "ch6.p3": "This plot also dismantles a pervasive myth: <strong>microwave ovens operate at 2.45 GHz not because it is a \"water resonance frequency\"</strong>. The first rotational line of isolated water vapor sits at <strong>22.235 GHz</strong>, while in 25 °C liquid water, hydrogen-bonding damping broadens the dielectric loss peak ε''(f) across <strong>18–20 GHz</strong> (around 19.6 GHz). If an oven operated at 20 GHz, absorption would be so ferocious that penetration depth would drop to <strong>0.46 mm</strong>—charring the outer millimetre while the center remained frozen solid. Tuning far off-peak to 2.45 GHz (in the unlicensed ISM band) gives moderate absorption with a ~<strong>1.9 cm</strong> power penetration depth in pure water (slightly shallower in salty food), heating evenly from outside to core.",
  "ch6.p4": "Crossing 6.25 THz upward, thermal infrared at 10 μm (30 THz) lands squarely on vibrational-rotational absorption bands of CO₂ and water vapor, trapping outgoing heat within Earth's greenhouse layer (see <a href=\"../../../earth/climate/why-the-planet-warms/#ch2\">A Little CO₂ · Why the Planet Warms</a>). Past visible light (~2–3 eV), frequencies above ~10 eV enter <strong>ionizing radiation</strong>: a single 13.5 nm extreme-ultraviolet (EUV) photon carries <strong>91.8 eV</strong>, enough to dismantle organic molecules; in stark contrast, a 1.8 GHz phone photon carries merely <strong>7.4 μeV</strong>, more than a million times below the ionization threshold.",
  "ch6.p5": "Regarding the frequently asked question of <strong>cell phone radiation safety</strong>, the International Commission on Non-Ionizing Radiation Protection (ICNIRP 2020) limits head and torso localized Specific Absorption Rate (SAR) to <strong>2.0 W/kg</strong> (averaged over 10 g of tissue; the US FCC sets 1.6 W/kg over 1 g), primarily to prevent tissue temperature increases exceeding 1 °C. In September 2024, a World Health Organization (WHO) commissioned systematic review by Karipidis et al. published in <em>Environment International</em> synthesized extensive population studies from 1994 to 2022, concluding that <strong>no association was found between mobile phone use (including heavy use over 10+ years) and risks of glioma, meningioma, or acoustic neuroma</strong>; the concurrent COSMOS prospective cohort study tracking over 250,000 individuals reported consistent results. Objectively noted: the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) 2011 classification of radiofrequency electromagnetic fields as Group 2B (\"possibly carcinogenic\") has not yet been formally updated, and some advocacy groups (such as ICBE-EMF) continue to contest the thermal-only safety paradigm and epidemiological review methodologies.",
  "ch6.limit": "Model boundaries: The liquid water curve uses a single Debye relaxation model at 25 °C (real food contains salts, which introduce ionic conduction losses at low frequencies); ionization thresholds vary by molecule (mostly between 10 and 13.6 eV).",

  /* ── 07 ── */
  "ch7.title": "Earth's atmosphere opens a few skylights for electromagnetic waves, blocking all the rest",
  "ch7.law": "Oxygen, water vapor, carbon dioxide, ozone, and the upper ionosphere act like bandpass filters; where astronomers build observatories on the ground versus launch them into space depends entirely on where atmospheric windows open.",
  "ch7.p1": "Light and radio waves travel effortlessly across vacuum, but plunging into Earth's hundred-kilometre-thick atmosphere subjects them to a ruthless filter. At low frequencies, free electrons in the upper ionosphere exhibit a critical plasma frequency f₀F₂ (typically between <strong>3–12 MHz</strong>, fluctuating with day, night, and solar activity): shortwave broadcasts below this frequency bounce off the layer back to Earth for intercontinental communication, while higher frequencies pierce through toward satellites.",
  "ch7.p2": "Above the ionosphere lies the expansive <strong>radio window</strong> (about 15 MHz to hundreds of GHz): zenith attenuation at 10 GHz is only ~0.01–0.02 dB, with zenith transmittance exceeding <strong>99%</strong>. Pushing higher, however, water vapor lines open absorption spikes at <strong>22.235 GHz</strong> and <strong>183.31 GHz</strong>, while molecular oxygen erects an opaque ~13.5 dB wall at <strong>60 GHz</strong> (plunging zenith transmission to ~<strong>4%</strong>), sealing off the 0.3–20 THz expanse. Only in the mid-infrared at <strong>8–14 μm</strong> and <strong>3–5 μm</strong> do two narrow slits reopen, followed by the brilliant <strong>0.38–0.75 μm visible window</strong>; past <strong>300 nm</strong>, the Hartley ozone band in the stratosphere and upper-atmospheric N₂/O₂ shut down ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays entirely.",
  "ch7.model.title": "Experiment 07 · Earth Zenith Atmospheric Transmission Spectrum (ITU-R P.676 and Standard IR/Ozone Data)",
  "ch7.model.how": "Drag the frequency cursor and tune sea-level water vapor density ρ_w and ionospheric critical frequency f₀F₂ to observe how millimeter-wave windows (e.g. 94 GHz) and terahertz close with humidity, and why the 60 GHz oxygen wall remains immune to moisture.",
  "ch7.model.aria": "Earth atmospheric zenith transmittance curve from 1 MHz to 10^18 Hz highlighting radio, infrared, and visible windows",
