  "ch7.say.body": "At <em>{f}</em> (λ = {lam}) with water vapor {rho} g/m³, zenith transmittance is <em>{t}%</em> (attenuation {db} dB): {why}",
  "ch7.why.iono": "Below the ionospheric f₀F₂, waves reflect off upper plasma back to Earth—ideal for long-range shortwave broadcasting, but unable to reach satellite orbits.",
  "ch7.why.open": "Inside an atmospheric transmission window, letting over 70% of radiation reach the surface directly.",
  "ch7.why.mid": "On the edge of an atmospheric window or in a moderate vapor absorption band; ground observations suffer noticeable attenuation from moisture and scattering.",
  "ch7.why.shut": "The atmosphere is almost completely opaque: cosmic signals cannot be received on the ground here, forcing telescopes to high-altitude deserts (like ALMA) or into orbit.",
  "ch7.p3": "This is why the 5,000-metre Atacama Desert in Chile hosts premier millimeter and submillimeter radio telescope arrays (leaving most water vapor beneath them), and why Hubble, Webb, the Chandra X-ray Observatory, and EUV lithography machines (which must be kept under high vacuum) cannot function for a second in air.",
  "ch7.limit": "Model boundaries: This dataset combines ITU-R P.676 standard sea-level atmosphere parameters (1013.25 hPa, 15 °C, reference vapor 7.5 g/m³) with typical mid-latitude infrared/ozone/ionospheric cutoffs to depict total vertical zenith transmittance, omitting liquid cloud droplets and rain fade.",

  /* ── 08 ── */
  "ch8.title": "Encoding information onto waves, and calculating what survives 25 billion kilometres away",
  "ch8.law": "A pure sine wave carries energy but no new information; one must alter its amplitude or phase within bandwidth B using AM, FM, or QAM, and whether the signal cuts through noise is decided by the Friis transmission formula and Shannon capacity.",
  "ch8.p1": "A perfectly periodic sine wave's future phase can be predicted with absolute certainty—it conveys zero new information. To transmit voice, photos, or code, the transmitter must break that symmetry: modulating amplitude (AM), instantaneous frequency (FM), or encoding symbols simultaneously in amplitude and phase (QAM). The faster the modulation, the wider the occupied bandwidth B (see <a href=\"../../../signal/sampling/modulation/#step0\">Modulation · Shipping Signals on a Carrier</a>, <a href=\"../../../signal/transforms/fourier/#step0\">The Fourier Transform · One Interaction at a Time</a>, and <a href=\"../../../tech/devices/inside-a-phone/#radio\">Ten Ideas Inside One Phone</a>).",
  "ch8.p2": "Next comes the ultimate engineering question: of the transmitted power P_tx, how much survives at the receiver? Combining the antenna gains G_tx and G_rx from Chapter 4, the spherical spreading 1/(4πd²) from Chapter 5, and the receiving antenna's effective aperture λ²/(4π) yields the foundation of wireless communications—the <strong>Friis transmission formula</strong>: P_rx (dBm) = P_tx + G_tx + G_rx − 20 log₁₀(4πd/λ) − L_atm. Subtracting thermal noise N = k_B T_sys B reveals how many bits per second can theoretically cross the channel (Shannon capacity C = B log₂(1 + SNR)).",
  "ch8.model.title": "Experiment 08 · Four Real-World Link Budgets and Modulation Waveforms (Wi-Fi / Cellular / GPS / Voyager 1)",
  "ch8.model.how": "Toggle between four real communication presets and scale distance to see how gains and free-space path loss push power down by hundreds of decibels above; switch AM / FM / 16-QAM below to observe how carriers encode symbols under current SNR.",
  "ch8.model.aria1": "Friis link budget waterfall diagram displaying transmit power, antenna gains, path loss, and thermal noise floor",
  "ch8.model.aria2": "Modulation waveforms for AM, FM, and 16-QAM varying under current link SNR",
