  "ch8.say.body": "Link <em>{name}</em> (frequency {f}, distance {d}, one-way light time {lt}): free-space spreading takes <em>{fspl} dB</em>, leaving merely <em>{prx} dBm</em> ({pw} W) at the receiving antenna, with an SNR of {snr} dB above thermal noise. {note}",
  "ch8.note.wifi": "Ten metres from the router, SNR exceeds 40 dB, effortlessly supporting high-throughput 16-QAM / 4096-QAM at hundreds of Mbps.",
  "ch8.note.cell": "Under 1 km urban clutter, pulling distance out to several kilometres or stepping into an elevator rapidly throttles throughput via path loss and building penetration.",
  "ch8.note.gps": "Notice that GPS received power is −127.2 dBm, fully 18 dB below the thermal noise floor (−108.9 dBm) across its 2 MHz bandwidth—the signal is buried deep beneath noise, relying on spread-spectrum correlation to extract the bits.",
  "ch8.note.voyager": "As of late 2026, Voyager 1 is ~173 AU away (~1 light-day); with a 22.4 W transmitter no more powerful than a refrigerator bulb, traversing 319 dB of space loss leaves less than 1 attowatt (10⁻¹⁸ W) at the 70 m DSN dish on Earth; using a 20 K cryogenic receiver to narrow bandwidth to 160 Hz secures an SNR of ~8.4 dB.",
  "ch8.p3": "Compare the two miracles in the default presets: <strong>GPS L1 (1575.42 MHz, 20,200 km)</strong> reaches a phone antenna at only <strong>−127.2 dBm</strong> (−157.2 dBW), buried <strong>18.3 dB</strong> below thermal noise, requiring pseudo-random spread-spectrum codes to correlate and integrate the 50 bps navigation message out of 2.046 MHz wideband noise; meanwhile, as of 2026, <strong>Voyager 1</strong>, cruising at <strong>~173 AU</strong> (about 25.9 billion km, where light takes a full day to travel), transmits with merely <strong>22.4 W</strong> (43.5 dBm) against an immense <strong>319.2 dB</strong> free-space loss, yet Earth's 70-metre DSN dishes and cryogenic low-noise amplifiers narrow bandwidth to 160 Hz to sustain crisp communication at about <strong>+8.4 dB</strong> SNR.",
  "ch8.p4": "Shannon's formula also clarifies why high-order modulation schemes (like 16-QAM or Wi-Fi 7's 4096-QAM) work only at short range: constellation points are packed so closely that small noise fluctuations cause symbol errors; as distance grows and SNR plunges, communication systems gracefully fall back to robust BPSK.",
  "ch8.limit": "Model boundaries: The Friis formula assumes line-of-sight far-field propagation; the cellular base station model includes 15 dB empirical building clutter without multipath fast fading; Voyager 1's 3.7 m and DSN's 70 m dishes both estimate gain with 55% aperture efficiency.",

  /* ── 09 ── */
  "ch9.title": "Preventing waves from scattering in all directions: waveguides lock light, arrays aim the beam",
  "ch9.law": "There are only two ways to beat 1/r² spreading: trap the wave inside a glass thread via total internal reflection (dropping 1550 nm loss to 0.18 dB/km), or use larger apertures and phased-array interference to focus energy into a razor-sharp beam.",
  "ch9.p1": "Given how merciless Chapter 8's 1/r² free-space loss is, why doesn't terrestrial internet rely entirely on radio? Because glass fibres use <strong>total internal reflection</strong> to trap light inside a core less than ten microns across, guiding it in one dimension. Standard single-mode silica fibre (ITU-T G.652.D) has core index n₁ ≈ 1.4682 and cladding index n₂ ≈ 1.4629; as long as the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle of <strong>85.13°</strong> (numerical aperture NA = 0.125), light skims along the core without leaking. Across the near-infrared spectrum, shorter wavelengths are crippled by Rayleigh scattering (∝ 1/λ⁴) while longer wavelengths collide with the molecular vibrational absorption wall of silica, with a residual OH⁻ water peak at 1383 nm—leaving <strong>1550 nm</strong> nestled right in the valley floor at a golden minimum of <strong>0.18–0.19 dB/km</strong>, retaining well over half its power after ten kilometres.",
  "ch9.p2": "When waves must travel across vacuum or air, focusing energy into a narrow beam cannot escape the <strong>diffraction limit θ ≈ 1.22 λ/D</strong>: the shorter the wavelength and the larger the aperture D, the sharper the beam (see <a href=\"../../waves/interference/#step0\">Interference · A Pattern of Loud and Still</a>). DSN's 70-metre antenna forms a beam of only <strong>0.036°</strong> at 8.415 GHz; the James Webb Space Telescope's 6.5-metre mirror resolves angles down to <strong>0.077 arcsec</strong> at 2 μm near-infrared; and lithography tools printing nanometre features on cutting-edge chips must shrink wavelength to 13.5 nm EUV while maximizing numerical aperture NA—the central optical bottleneck in shrinking chip features. What if you cannot mechanically rotate a multi-tonne antenna? The solution is to arrange N antenna elements in an array and apply a small calibrated phase shift Δφ to each—steering the main beam electronically to any angle θ₀ via wave interference: <strong>phased-array beamforming</strong> in 5G base stations, Starlink, and modern radars.",
  "ch9.model.title": "Experiment 09 · Phased Array Electronic Beam Steering (Top) and Silica Fibre 1550 nm Window (Bottom)",
  "ch9.model.how": "Adjust the element count N, steering angle θ₀, and element spacing d/λ (watch for grating lobes appearing at wide angles when d/λ &gt; 0.5); then drag the fibre wavelength slider below to compare attenuation at 850, 1310, and 1550 nm.",
  "ch9.model.aria1": "2-D wavefront interference pattern and polar beam plot of an N-element phased array",
  "ch9.model.aria2": "Standard single-mode silica fibre attenuation spectrum from 850 to 1700 nm highlighting the 1550 nm window",
